---
import SimulatorLayout from '../../layouts/SimulatorLayout.astro';
import FoodWebSim from '../../simulators/foodweb/FoodWebSim.astro';
import { foodWebQuiz } from '../../data/quizzes/foodweb';

const toc = [
  { id: 'chains', label: 'Food chains' },
  { id: 'levels', label: 'Trophic levels' },
  { id: 'webs', label: 'Food webs' },
  { id: 'energy', label: 'Energy flow and the 10 % rule' },
  { id: 'cascades', label: 'Knock-on effects and trophic cascades' },
  { id: 'decomposers', label: 'Decomposers' },
  { id: 'humans', label: 'Humans and food webs' },
  { id: 'terms', label: 'Key terms' },
  { id: 'misconceptions', label: 'Common misconceptions' },
  { id: 'takeaways', label: 'Key takeaways' },
];

const references = [
  { text: 'Clark, M. A., Douglas, M., & Choi, J. (2018). Biology 2e, Section 46.2: Energy Flow through Ecosystems. OpenStax.', url: 'https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/46-2-energy-flow-through-ecosystems' },
  { text: 'Ripple, W. J., & Beschta, R. L. (2012). Trophic cascades in Yellowstone: The first 15 years after wolf reintroduction. Biological Conservation, 145(1), 205–213.' },
  { text: 'Lindeman, R. L. (1942). The trophic-dynamic aspect of ecology. Ecology, 23(4), 399–417.' },
  { text: 'Paine, R. T. (1966). Food web complexity and species diversity. The American Naturalist, 100(910), 65–75.' },
];
---

<SimulatorLayout
  branch="zoology"
  topic="food-webs"
  description="Interactive food web simulator: remove, reduce or boost species in a woodland ecosystem and watch the knock-on effects ripple through producers, herbivores and predators."
  toc={toc}
  quiz={foodWebQuiz}
  references={references}
>
  <Fragment slot="intro">
    <p>
      In nature, everything is connected by who eats whom. Take away one species and the effects can spread through the
      whole ecosystem, sometimes in surprising ways. Explore a woodland food web of 14 species and test what happens when
      you remove a predator, spread a disease or wipe out the insects.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <FoodWebSim slot="sim" />

  <Fragment slot="instructions">
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Click a species</strong> to select it. Its food and predators light up, and the graph shows their populations.</li>
      <li>Press <strong>Remove</strong> and watch the web adjust over the following years. Species that run out of food die out.</li>
      <li>Try <strong>Disease</strong> (halve the population) or <strong>Introduce more</strong> (double it), and see whether it recovers.</li>
      <li>Experiments to try: remove the wolves; remove both insects; remove the grass; remove the mice.</li>
      <li>Press <strong>Reset</strong> to restore the ecosystem.</li>
    </ol>
  </Fragment>

  <h2 id="chains">Food chains</h2>
  <p>
    A <strong>food chain</strong> shows how energy passes from one organism to another as each is eaten: for example,
    grass → rabbit → fox. The arrows point in the direction energy flows, from the food to the eater. Every food chain
    starts with a <strong>producer</strong>, an organism such as a plant or alga that makes its own food from sunlight by
    photosynthesis.
  </p>

  <h2 id="levels">Trophic levels</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Producers</strong> make food from sunlight (grass, oak, berry bushes).</li>
    <li><strong>Primary consumers</strong> are herbivores that eat producers (grasshoppers, rabbits, deer).</li>
    <li><strong>Secondary consumers</strong> eat herbivores (frogs, snakes); omnivores such as foxes and songbirds eat both plants and animals.</li>
    <li><strong>Tertiary consumers</strong> or <strong>top predators</strong> are not usually eaten by anything else (hawks, wolves).</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="webs">Food webs</h2>
  <p>
    Real animals rarely eat just one thing, so food chains link together into a <strong>food web</strong>. A fox eats
    rabbits, mice, birds and berries; a mouse is eaten by snakes, foxes and hawks. Generalists with many food sources can
    switch when one runs short, which makes them resilient. <strong>Specialists</strong>, such as a frog that eats only
    insects, are much more vulnerable.
  </p>

  <h2 id="energy">Energy flow and the 10 % rule</h2>
  <p>
    At each step along a food chain, most energy is used for movement, growth, keeping warm and other life processes, or
    is lost as heat and waste. Typically only about <strong>10 %</strong> is passed on to the next level. That is why
    there is much more plant material than herbivores, and many more herbivores than predators, and why food chains rarely
    have more than four or five links. Drawn as a chart, this makes an <strong>energy pyramid</strong>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="cascades">Knock-on effects and trophic cascades</h2>
  <p>
    Because species are linked, a change to one affects many others. Removing a predator lets its prey increase, which
    can then overeat their own food: a <strong>trophic cascade</strong>. When wolves were reintroduced to Yellowstone
    National Park in 1995, elk numbers fell and changed their behaviour, letting willows and aspens regrow along rivers,
    which in turn helped beavers and songbirds. Species with an unusually large effect for their numbers are called
    <strong>keystone species</strong>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="decomposers">Decomposers</h2>
  <p>
    Every food web relies on <strong>decomposers</strong>, mainly fungi and bacteria, along with detritivores such as
    earthworms. They break down dead organisms and waste, releasing nutrients such as nitrogen and phosphorus back into
    the soil for producers to reuse. Without them, nutrients would stay locked in dead matter.
  </p>

  <h2 id="humans">Humans and food webs</h2>
  <p>
    Overfishing, hunting, habitat loss, pesticides and invasive species all disrupt food webs. Some pollutants, such as
    the pesticide DDT or mercury, are not broken down and become more concentrated at each level of a food chain
    (<strong>biomagnification</strong>). In the 1960s this thinned the eggshells of eagles and falcons, almost wiping them
    out until DDT was banned.
  </p>

  <h2 id="terms">Key terms</h2>
  <dl>
    <dt>Trophic level</dt>
    <dd>A step in a food chain, such as producer or primary consumer.</dd>
    <dt>Keystone species</dt>
    <dd>A species whose effect on its ecosystem is much larger than its numbers suggest.</dd>
    <dt>Trophic cascade</dt>
    <dd>A chain of effects through several levels, caused by a change at one level.</dd>
  </dl>

  <h2 id="misconceptions">Common misconceptions</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>“Arrows show who eats whom.”</strong> They show the direction energy flows: from the food to the eater.</li>
    <li><strong>“Removing a predator helps everything it ate.”</strong> Prey may boom and then crash after overeating their food.</li>
    <li><strong>“Top predators are the most important.”</strong> Every level matters; without producers and decomposers nothing survives.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="takeaways">Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Food chains link into food webs; arrows show the flow of energy.</li>
    <li>Only about 10 % of energy passes between levels, so higher levels support fewer organisms.</li>
    <li>Changing one population causes knock-on effects throughout the web.</li>
    <li>Specialists are more vulnerable than generalists; decomposers recycle nutrients for everyone.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="muted">Model note: populations are shown relative to normal levels and respond to the average amount of food and predators. Real food webs involve many more species, seasons and behaviours.</p>
</SimulatorLayout>
